Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) can achieve high classification accuracy while grounding their predictions on background or irrelevant image regions.
	Furthermore, external input perturbations can alter both what the models predict and what evidence they cosider for making the predictions.
	Accuracy-based evaluation captures neither problem.
	We propose two complementary model-level metrics that integrate predictive performance with explanation quality.
	The Explainable Trustworthy Index (ETI) combines classification accuracy with the Localization Mask Overlap Score (LMOS).
	LMOS is the proportion of salient Grad-CAM activation that falls within the ground-truth object mask. The Explainable Robustness Index (ERI) measures how well a model preserves both accuracy and LMOS under input perturbations, relative to its behavior on clean images.
	We evaluate both metrics on four real-world leaf and flower image datasets, using eight CNN architectures and nine perturbations, including noise, blur, and occlusion. The results show that accuracy and explanation quality frequently diverge.
	On the EPLI dataset, a Custom CNN attains 99.45\% accuracy, yet only 19\% of its salient activation falls on the leaf, giving it the lowest ETI (0.5922).
	Across all the datasets, DenseNet121 achieves the highest accuracy but the highest ETI on none, while Xception obtains the highest ERI on all four.
	Predictive and explanation stability also diverge: under contrast perturbation, the Custom CNN largely preserves its accuracy while its object focus shifts substantially, whereas ResNet50 shows the opposite pattern.
	ETI and ERI thus expose differences in reliability and robustness that accuracy alone conceals, providing complementary criteria for model evaluation and selection.
	
